  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Tanzania, the informal economy comprises nearly half of the economy and employs a significant portion of the workforce, particularly women. However, workers in this sector face substantial challenges, including limited access to social and labor protections, decent work opportunities, and gender equality. Vulnerable groups such as women, youth, and those in rural and informal economies are particularly affected by these deficits.

This Country Programme Outcome (CPO) aims to strengthen the institutional capacities of workers&apos; organizations, enabling them to organize and represent workers in the informal and rural economies more effectively. The focus is on empowering the Trade Union Congress of Tanzania (TUCTA) and the Zanzibar Trade Union Congress (ZATUC) to improve their ability to promote international labor standards, foster gender equality, and advocate for decent work for all. The intervention prioritizes increasing membership recruitment, enhancing collective bargaining, and addressing issues such as freedom of association, labor rights, and gender equity in the informal economy.

By supporting workers&#x2019; organizations, the initiative contributes to the implementation of relevant international labor standards and the development of inclusive care policies. This aligns with the Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP) and global goals under the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, focusing on eradicating poverty (SDG 1), promoting decent work (SDG 8), and reducing inequality (SDG 10).

The intervention anticipates long-term benefits for workers in the informal economy by fostering an inclusive and rights-based approach to labor and social protection in Tanzania.</narrative>
  </description>
